AUDIO: 'a bit different here. So, what does 1.0 actually mean here? Not that Mojo is finished. It more or less means stability. Their promise is that the code you write today won\'t break. That\'s'

[03:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what a major release like this is. But then you hit the first weird part. Mojo reached 1.1 before its compiler was even open source. The standard library had already opened in March 2024. The max kernels opened in 2025. But the'

[03:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'compiler, the actual thing doing the compiling, that stayed proprietary until August 18th of this year. Apache 2.0. And that\'s important because just seven days earlier when Mojo 1.0 was shipped,'

[03:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it was still closed. And that answers one of the biggest criticisms Mojo has had in three straight years. You used to be able to say, "Sure, but the compiler is closed." We can\'t really say that anymore, which sounds like, okay, this'

[04:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
